My experience is that for foodstuffs not to rot,

1) they are lichen or lizard corpses, or 2) they are
kept in ice chests

All other varieties of food rot with great
regularity for my PCs: fruits, leafy vegetables,
pancakes, foo-rations, wafers.

Even tinned stuff sometimes produces the "this food
is rotten" message. I don't have enough experience
with ice chests, which I rarely encounter, to know
if food stored there eventually rots as well, just
more slowly.

It is of course more than possible that food is
merely _created_ rotten, rather than rotting over
time. Surely some corpses, that are rotten even
if you eat them in the minimum two ot three turns
possible (depending on autopickup setting), seem
to be _created_ rotten, since that is an illogical
quantity of time in which it would rot.

I'd have no way to know the difference from game
play between food rotting and being created rotten,
of course.

Also, food's being cursed seems to make it at least
most times rotten when eaten in that state.

xanthian, who tends to carry food for very long
times before eating it.

What non-corpse food seems not to do is to become
_tainted_, ever.



